There is an integer $D_0$ such that, for every fixed even $D\ge D_0$, the following assertion holds as $q$ tends to infinity through odd primes. Let $X_i$, $i\in I$, and $Y_j$, $j\in J$, be independent uniform elements of $\mathcal S_D$. For every set $B\subseteq\mathscr C$ and every choice of signs $\xi_{ij}\in\{1,-1\}$ on $B$, $$\mathbb E\prod_{(i,j)\in B}K_{\xi_{ij}}(X_i-Y_j)
 =\mathbb E_\eta\prod_{j\in J}
 \prod_{\substack{e=\{i,k\}\subset j\\(i,j),(k,j)\in B}}
 \bigl(1+c_0\eta_e\xi_{ij}\xi_{kj}\bigr)+o(1),
 \label{eq:rank-layer}$$ where the $\eta_e$, $e\in E$, are independent uniform signs. The error is uniform over all such $B$ and sign choices. The same assertion holds with $Y_j-X_i$ in place of $X_i-Y_j$.

Assume Equation <a href="#eq:kernel-zero-moments" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:kernel-zero-moments">[eq:kernel-zero-moments]</a> and fix an even dimension as in Lemma <a href="#lem:rank-layer" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:rank-layer">5</a>. For fixed type laws and activation laws, $$\begin{aligned}
 t_{I,J}(W_q)&=1+
 \sum_{a:I\to\mathcal A\,,\ b:J\to\mathcal B}
 \left(\prod_{i\in I}\pi_{a(i)}\right)
 \left(\prod_{j\in J}\nu_{b(j)}\right)C_A(a,b)+o(1).
 \label{eq:kernel-expansion}
\end{aligned}$$ The corresponding formula for $W_q^\top$ has type maps $b:I\to\mathcal B$, $a:J\to\mathcal A$ and the corner factors $A_{a(j)b(i)}$.

Let $F=(P\sqcup Q,L)$ be a finite bipartite graph for which color refinement, starting with the two part colors, becomes discrete within each part. There are real vertex scores $s_t$, $t\in P\sqcup Q$, and pair scores $s_{ab}$, $(a,b)\in P\times Q$, such that the identity is the unique maximizer of $$\mathcal Q(f)=\sum_{u\in P\sqcup Q}s_{f(u)}
              +\sum_{\{u,v\}\in L,\ u\in P}s_{f(u)f(v)}
 \label{eq:kernel-pinning-score}$$ over *all* maps $f$ preserving the two parts. In particular the maximum has a positive gap from every other such map; no injectivity or adjacency-preservation condition is imposed.

For $0\leq k\leq n$, the number of $k$-dimensional subspaces of $\mathbb F_q^n$ is $$\label{eq:transverse-grassmann}
 \genfrac{[}{]}{0pt}{}{n}{k}_q
 =\prod_{a=0}^{k-1}\frac{q^n-q^a}{q^k-q^a}
 =q^{k(n-k)}(1+O(q^{-1})).$$ For a uniform matrix $M\in\mathcal S_n$, with $n\geq 1$, $$\mathbb P(M\text{ is nonsingular})=1-O(q^{-1}),\qquad
 \mathbb P(\chi(\det M)=\xi)=\frac12+O(q^{-1})
 \quad(\xi\in\{+1,-1\}).$$ For $1\leq k\leq n$, the probability of rank $k$ and prescribed discriminant sign is $$\label{eq:transverse-signed-rank}
 \left(\frac12+O(q^{-1})\right)q^{-b(n-k)}.$$ Finally, for $0\leq u\leq n$, $$\label{eq:transverse-nullity}
 \mathbb P(\dim\ker M\geq u)=O(q^{-b(u)}).$$

Let $r\geq1$. A nondegenerate symmetric form on a $2r$-dimensional space over $\mathbb F_q$ has an $r$-dimensional totally isotropic subspace if and only if its determinant character is $c_0=\chi((-1)^r)$. Such a form is a direct orthogonal sum of $r$ hyperbolic planes. The total number of its $r$-dimensional totally isotropic subspaces is $$\label{eq:transverse-isotropic-count}
 N_r=\prod_{a=0}^{r-1}(1+q^a)
 =2q^{r(r-1)/2}(1+O(q^{-1})).$$ The count includes both families of maximal isotropic subspaces.

Let $\mathcal U$ be any nonempty family of distinct $r$-subspaces of $\mathbb F_q^D$, with $r\geq1$, and let $Q$ be a uniform symmetric form. For $\xi\in\{+1,-1\}$ put $$f_\xi(Q)=\frac1{|\mathcal U|}\sum_{U\in\mathcal U}
 \mathbf 1_{\{Q|_U\text{ nonsingular of sign }\xi\}}.$$ Uniformly over the family $\mathcal U$, $$\label{eq:transverse-restriction-error}
 \mathbb E\left|f_\xi(Q)-\frac12\right|
 =O\left(q^{-1/2}+|\mathcal U|^{-1/2}\right).$$ The same bound holds after conditioning $Q$ to be nonsingular, or conditioning both $Q$ and $Q-L$ to be nonsingular, uniformly over every fixed symmetric form $L$.

Suppose $B\in\mathcal S_{2r}$ is nonsingular and $A\in\mathcal S_{2r}$ has rank $r$. Then $$\label{eq:transverse-projection}
 \mathop{\mathrm{rank}}(B-A)=r\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad AB^{-1}A=A.$$ If $A$ and $A-B$ have discriminant signs $\xi_1,\xi_2$, respectively, under these conditions, then $$\label{eq:transverse-pair-sign}
 \chi(\det B)=c_0\xi_1\xi_2.$$

For every fixed even $D\geq12$, every activity pattern $B\subseteq\mathscr C$, and every prescribed set of incidence signs, $$\label{eq:transverse-limit}
 \mathbb E\left[\mathbf 1_{\mathcal T}
       \prod_{(i,j)\in B}K_{\xi_{ij}}(X_i-Y_j)\right]
 =\mathbb E_\eta\prod_{j\in J}\prod_{\{i,k\}\subseteq A_j}
       (1+c_0\eta_{\{i,k\}}\xi_{ij}\xi_{kj})+o(1),$$ where the $\eta_e$ are independent uniform signs. At each fixed $D$, the error is uniform over all activity patterns and incidence signs.

Let $B\subseteq\mathscr C$ be a set of active incidences, with prescribed signs $\xi_{ij}$. Let $F$ be any nonnegative function of the $35$ Lagrangians indexed by $I\sqcup J$. For independent uniform matrices $X_i,Y_j\in\mathcal S_D$ and independent uniform Lagrangians $L_i,M_j$, $$\begin{aligned}
 &\mathbb E\left[
 F((\Gamma_{X_i})_i,(\Gamma_{Y_j})_j)
 \prod_{(i,j)\in B}K_{\xi_{ij}}(X_i-Y_j)\right]\notag\\
 &\qquad\le
 \beta_D^{-35}\prod_{(i,j)\in B}\frac{\pi_{D,r}}{p_{\xi_{ij}}}
 \ \mathbb E\left[F((L_i)_i,(M_j)_j)
 \prod_{(i,j)\in B}J(L_i,M_j)\right].
 \label{eq:orbits-chart-comparison}
\end{aligned}$$ The multiplier is bounded in terms of $D$ alone for all sufficiently large $q$, uniformly in the active set and its signs. In particular, this comparison applies with $F$ the indicator that some pair in $E$ has positive intersection dimension, or the indicator of any specified intersection profile.

Let $A_1,A_2,A_3$ be arbitrary subspaces of a vector space, put $Q_i=A_j\cap A_k$, and put $V=A_1\cap A_2\cap A_3$. The image of the cyclic difference map on symmetric forms is exactly $$\label{eq:planted-difference-image}
 \left\{(D_1,D_2,D_3):
       D_1|_V+D_2|_V+D_3|_V=0\right\}.$$ Its codimension in the product of the three form spaces on the $Q_i$ is $b(\dim V)$. Independent uniform $F_i$ give the uniform law on this image.

For a local profile $(h_1,h_2,h_3,c)$, we choose one pointwise gain exponent independent of $q$ as follows. Let $\mathcal U$ be the union, over all odd prime fields and all ordered orbits of this profile, of the parameter tuples arising from actual quadruples $(L_1,L_2,L_3,Y)$ with the stated point profile and $\dim(L_i\cap Y)=r$, using Equations <a href="#eq:planted-reduced-parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:planted-reduced-parameters">[eq:planted-reduced-parameters]</a> and <a href="#eq:planted-subspace-parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:planted-subspace-parameters">[eq:planted-subspace-parameters]</a>. Their integer entries are bounded at fixed $D$, so $\mathcal U$ is finite. If $\mathcal U$ is empty, the face factor is zero over every odd prime field. Otherwise the face factor with three active incidences is $O(q^g)$ uniformly over all triples in the profile, where $$\label{eq:planted-gain}
 g=\max_{\text{tuple}\in\mathcal U}\left\{
 g_C+\sum_i b(s_i)-\delta P-\sum_i p_i^2-vw
                   -\sum_i b(t_i)+b(v)\right\},$$ so the selected exponent is independent of $q$.*


*Every tuple in $\mathcal U$ satisfies the following necessary constraints: $$\label{eq:planted-feasibility}
 \begin{gathered}
 0\le u\le\min(c,r),\quad 0\le r'=r-u\le d,
 \quad |\delta|\le c,\quad d-\delta=2r',\\
 P+T=\sum_i s_i\le d,\qquad
 0\le t_i\le p_i+\delta,\qquad
 0\le v\le p_i+\delta\quad(1\le i\le3),\qquad w\ge0.
 \end{gathered}$$ In particular $T\le P+3\delta$ and $d+3\delta\ge0$. These conditions are not claimed sufficient for realizability.

For two active incidences whose point Lagrangians meet in dimension $h$, a valid gain exponent is $$\label{eq:planted-two-active-gain}
 g_2(h)
 =\max_{\substack{p+t=h\,;\ 0\le t\le p\le r}}
      \{b(h)-p^2-b(t)\}
 =b(\lfloor h/2\rfloor).$$ For zero or one active incidence, the face factor is exactly one.

For every prescribed profile, its probability is $O_D(q^{-\mathcal C})$, where $$\label{eq:global-cost}
 \mathcal C=\frac12\left(
 \sum_{j\in J}C_j-\sum_{e\in E}(1-a_e)b(h_e)\right).$$

Let $h=\max_{e\subset j}h_e$. If $j$ has at most two active incidences, then $$\label{eq:tail-lower-active}
 S_j\ge0,
 \qquad S_j\ge\frac{h^2}{24}-\frac h{12}.$$ Equality $S_j=0$ requires $c_j=0$ and either all three pair dimensions zero or exactly one pair dimension equal to $2$. The latter case requires exactly two active incidences, at that pair’s endpoints.

There is an even-dimensional threshold such that, for every even $D$ above it, every feasible profile and every activity set for which $\sum_jg_j\ge\mathcal C$ has the following properties: $$\label{eq:tail-residual-profile}
 c_j=0\quad(j\in J),\qquad h_e\in\{0,2\}\quad(e\in E),
 \qquad\sum_jg_j\le\frac23\sum_{e\in E}b(h_e).$$

For every sufficiently large even $D=2r$ and every activity set $B\subseteq\mathscr C$, $$\label{eq:tail-vanishing}
 \mathbb E_{L,Y}\left[
 \mathbf 1_{\{\exists e=\{i,k\}\in E:\,\dim(L_i\cap L_k)>0\}}
 \prod_{(i,j)\in B}J(L_i,Y_j)\right]=o(1)$$ as $q\to\infty$ through odd primes, with $D$ fixed. The dimension threshold can be chosen for all activity sets simultaneously.
